{"A":"Investigate replacing dense matrix exponentiation with Kitamasa/characteristic-polynomial exponentiation in O(N² log H). Preserve the exact recurrence and modulo result for every valid input; verify the coefficient order against the base cases and handle N=1 and H=N+1 correctly. Compare end-to-end time with the fastest measured dense candidate, not just the polynomial kernel.","B":"Optimize the dense multiplication fallback for the ARM64 four-core target. Explore cache-friendly i/k/j tiling, contiguous output updates, and safe modular-reduction intervals, using the measured 0.008872-second SIMD/parallel candidate as the comparator. The measured row-tiled and transposed alternatives were slower, so accept changes only when the complete program improves and remains correct for all valid inputs.","C":"Study exponentiation overhead and parallel scheduling. In particular, avoid the final matrix square in the current power loop when that square cannot affect the result, and assess whether the returned matrix copy can be avoided. Benchmark serial versus OpenMP thresholds and thread settings against the measured fastest candidate; a separate four-thread parallel-region variant measured slower, so do not assume parallelism helps at every dimension.","G":"Integrate candidates from A, B, and C, but measure each assembled program: their gains are not additive, and integration may be slower than its inputs. The baseline measured 0.022342 seconds; the fastest individual candidate measured 0.008872 seconds and passed four correctness cases. Use that candidate as a comparator, retain only verified improvements, preserve correctness across all valid inputs, and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source files outside the assigned integration work.","analysis":"The source uses dense O(N³ log H) matrix exponentiation for a recurrence with N≤1020 and H≤10⁹, making recurrence-specific O(N² log H) exponentiation a promising independent direction. Among measured candidates, SIMD dot-product chunks with static row parallelism measured 0.008872 seconds, faster than the measured row-tiled variants; another four-thread parallel-region variant was slower. The baseline measured 0.022342 seconds. Existing correctness evidence covers four cases, so it does not establish correctness for every valid input."}